 "notes": "Curve traced from Fig. 5 (figures/semi_full_all_upper_limits.png in the e-print), the 95% CL upper limits on the dark-photon/baryon coupling strength eps^2 vs dark photon mass. The headline fully-coherent (matched-filter) limit was used: red curve (low-frequency PSD estimation, ~8e-20 to 3.6e-18 eV) joined to the black curve (high-frequency, up to ~1.3e-14 eV); the weaker semi-coherent (magenta) curve was not used. The paper's eps is the gauge coupling to baryon (or B-L) number normalized by the electric charge, L = -eps*e*J^mu*A_mu (Eq. 2), so the canonical coupling g = e*eps = sqrt(4*pi*alpha)*sqrt(eps^2) = 0.30282*sqrt(eps^2) was emitted with the other charge assignment implicit (paper treats baryon and B-L couplings together; test masses are a single material so the signal is the residual phase-difference effect). Axis calibration from gridlines (x: decades 1e-19..1e-14 eV; y: 1e-14..1e-38 every 4 decades). The published limit curve oscillates rapidly (noise lines/non-stationarities), filling a band up to ~4-5 decades wide in eps^2 at the highest masses; the reported points are the per-mass-bin median of the painted curve (band center), so a digitisation following the band's lower or upper envelope could differ by more than a factor of 2 in g at m > ~3e-16 eV. Limits include the stochastic dark-matter-amplitude correction (factor 9 in eps^2) as published. Local DM density is not stated numerically in the paper (only rho_DM symbolically); 0.4 GeV/cm^3 is the standard value of the methodology it follows and is recorded here as the assumed density. 95% CL via Feldman-Cousins.",
